1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,133
Who Pays When You Buy Now, Pay Later?

2
00:00:03,373 --> 00:00:05,453
Cyber Monday, 2025.

3
00:00:05,453 --> 00:00:09,533
In a single day, Americans spent more than
one billion dollars online with

4
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:11,053
buy now, pay later.

5
00:00:11,053 --> 00:00:12,230
A record.

6
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:13,910
Pay in four.

7
00:00:13,910 --> 00:00:14,910
No interest.

8
00:00:14,550 --> 00:00:16,230
No fees, if you pay on time.

9
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:18,889
It sounds like a loan where
nobody gets paid.

10
00:00:19,449 --> 00:00:23,769
But one of these companies went public in
2025 at a value of about fifteen

11
00:00:23,769 --> 00:00:24,969
billion dollars.

12
00:00:24,969 --> 00:00:26,969
Somebody is paying for all of this.

13
00:00:26,969 --> 00:00:30,192
So we're going to follow one
purchase, and find out who.

14
00:00:30,352 --> 00:00:32,272
This is Billions and Bulls.

15
00:00:32,272 --> 00:00:34,192
Let's follow the money.

16
00:00:35,072 --> 00:00:37,472
Part 1 Four Little Payments

17
00:00:37,472 --> 00:00:39,712
Say you want a four-hundred-dollar jacket.

18
00:00:39,712 --> 00:00:41,574
At checkout, you see a new button.

19
00:00:41,814 --> 00:00:43,494
Pay in four.

20
00:00:43,814 --> 00:00:45,814
You pay one hundred dollars today.

21
00:00:45,814 --> 00:00:49,334
Then one hundred dollars every two
weeks, three more times.

22
00:00:49,334 --> 00:00:51,814
Six weeks later, the jacket is paid off.

23
00:00:51,814 --> 00:00:52,814
No interest.

24
00:00:52,534 --> 00:00:53,534
No credit card.

25
00:00:53,738 --> 00:00:57,338
Often, just a quick check that
takes a few seconds.

26
00:00:57,738 --> 00:00:59,258
The idea isn't new.

27
00:00:59,258 --> 00:01:02,487
Stores have sold things in
installments for more than a century.

28
00:01:02,647 --> 00:01:06,647
In the 1850s, the Singer company sold its
sewing machines for a few dollars

29
00:01:06,647 --> 00:01:07,647
a month.

30
00:01:07,367 --> 00:01:11,127
Families who could never pay the full
price at once suddenly could.

31
00:01:11,127 --> 00:01:13,939
Furniture, pianos and cars followed.

32
00:01:14,179 --> 00:01:16,019
What's new is the speed.

33
00:01:16,019 --> 00:01:17,619
And the scale.

34
00:01:18,099 --> 00:01:21,939
The modern version took off in the 2010s,
with companies like Klarna from

35
00:01:21,939 --> 00:01:25,884
Sweden, Afterpay from Australia, and
Affirm from the United States.

36
00:01:25,884 --> 00:01:31,404
In 2021, the payments company Square, now
called Block, agreed to buy Afterpay

37
00:01:31,404 --> 00:01:35,732
in a deal reportedly worth about
twenty-nine billion dollars.

38
00:01:35,732 --> 00:01:40,292
According to the Consumer Financial
Protection Bureau, buy now, pay later

39
00:01:40,292 --> 00:01:44,692
loans in the United States went from
about two billion dollars in 2019 to

40
00:01:44,692 --> 00:01:47,412
more than twenty-four billion in 2021.

41
00:01:47,572 --> 00:01:49,332
And it kept growing.

42
00:01:49,332 --> 00:01:54,612
Over the 2025 holiday season, Adobe says
Americans spent a record twenty

43
00:01:54,612 --> 00:01:58,149
billion dollars online with
buy now, pay later.

44
00:01:58,628 --> 00:02:00,548
It's not just a US thing.

45
00:02:00,548 --> 00:02:04,948
In the UK, the Financial Conduct Authority
says almost eleven million adults

46
00:02:04,948 --> 00:02:06,708
used it in 2024.

47
00:02:07,748 --> 00:02:09,963
And it's not just jackets anymore.

48
00:02:09,963 --> 00:02:15,162
In a 2026 survey by LendingTree,
twenty-nine percent of buy now, pay later

49
00:02:15,162 --> 00:02:17,962
users said they had used it
to buy groceries.

50
00:02:18,602 --> 00:02:19,826
So here's the puzzle.

51
00:02:19,986 --> 00:02:24,546
If you pay exactly four hundred dollars
for a four-hundred-dollar jacket,

52
00:02:24,546 --> 00:02:26,307
where does the money come from?

53
00:02:26,307 --> 00:02:29,987
The first answer is someone you
never see at checkout.

54
00:02:31,245 --> 00:02:33,325
Part 2 The Store Pays

55
00:02:33,325 --> 00:02:37,885
When you click pay in four, the buy now,
pay later company pays the store

56
00:02:37,885 --> 00:02:38,885
right away.

57
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:39,940
Not in four parts.

58
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:40,940
All at once.

59
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:42,660
Minus a fee.

60
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:45,220
That fee is the heart of the business.

61
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:46,930
It's called the merchant discount.

62
00:02:46,930 --> 00:02:51,650
In its 2022 report, the CFPB found that
the average merchant fee was about

63
00:02:51,650 --> 00:02:54,173
three point four percent in 2019.

64
00:02:54,173 --> 00:02:57,853
As more companies joined the market, it
fell to about two and a half percent

65
00:02:57,853 --> 00:02:59,293
by 2021.

66
00:02:59,293 --> 00:03:02,639
Depending on the store and the
plan, it can be higher.

67
00:03:03,119 --> 00:03:07,519
On our four-hundred-dollar jacket, two
and a half percent is ten dollars.

68
00:03:07,679 --> 00:03:09,039
Paid by the store.

69
00:03:09,039 --> 00:03:13,119
And for longer plans with zero percent
interest, the store usually pays

70
00:03:13,119 --> 00:03:14,119
more.

71
00:03:13,847 --> 00:03:17,688
Someone has to cover the cost of
waiting a whole year for the money.

72
00:03:17,688 --> 00:03:22,006
When the shopper pays no interest,
that someone is often the store.

73
00:03:22,326 --> 00:03:24,646
Remember our episode on credit cards?

74
00:03:24,646 --> 00:03:27,126
Stores already pay to accept cards.

75
00:03:27,126 --> 00:03:32,045
So why would a store pay a similar fee,
or a bigger one, to offer this?

76
00:03:32,684 --> 00:03:36,284
Because, according to the companies
selling it, people buy more.

77
00:03:36,284 --> 00:03:39,564
Affirm tells merchants they can
see much bigger orders.

78
00:03:39,564 --> 00:03:43,785
One figure it has used is a seventy
percent lift in average order value.

79
00:03:43,785 --> 00:03:48,745
A Klarna executive has claimed twenty
percent more sales completed at checkout.

80
00:03:48,745 --> 00:03:51,465
Those numbers come from the
companies themselves.

81
00:03:51,465 --> 00:03:53,326
But the logic is simple.

82
00:03:53,486 --> 00:03:57,726
A four-hundred-dollar jacket feels a lot
cheaper when the screen says one

83
00:03:57,726 --> 00:03:58,926
hundred.

84
00:03:59,326 --> 00:04:02,766
For Klarna, the biggest name in this
market, that's where most of the money

85
00:04:02,766 --> 00:04:03,766
comes from.

86
00:04:03,433 --> 00:04:07,672
In the year before its IPO, Klarna said
about three quarters of its revenue

87
00:04:07,672 --> 00:04:12,073
came from transaction and service fees,
most of it paid by merchants.

88
00:04:12,596 --> 00:04:16,596
So in the classic pay in four, the
shopper pays nothing extra.

89
00:04:16,596 --> 00:04:18,916
The store pays to make the sale easier.

90
00:04:18,916 --> 00:04:22,916
And just like with card fees, that cost
becomes part of what the store charges

91
00:04:22,916 --> 00:04:24,077
everyone.

92
00:04:24,557 --> 00:04:27,277
There's also a simple competitive reason.

93
00:04:27,277 --> 00:04:31,437
Once one store in a market offers four
easy payments, the store next door

94
00:04:31,437 --> 00:04:33,313
feels it has to offer them too.

95
00:04:33,552 --> 00:04:37,152
Nobody wants to be the only shop where a
four-hundred-dollar jacket still

96
00:04:37,152 --> 00:04:39,792
costs four hundred dollars today.

97
00:04:40,192 --> 00:04:43,090
But the store's fee is only
one part of the story.

98
00:04:43,330 --> 00:04:47,810
Because someone still has to hand over the
four hundred dollars on day one.

99
00:04:48,690 --> 00:04:52,051
Part 3 Where the Money Really Comes From

100
00:04:52,051 --> 00:04:54,054
Here's the thing about pay in four.

101
00:04:54,054 --> 00:04:55,733
The store gets paid today.

102
00:04:55,733 --> 00:04:57,413
You pay over six weeks.

103
00:04:57,413 --> 00:05:00,453
In between, someone has to
put up the cash.

104
00:05:00,773 --> 00:05:02,854
Sometimes it's the company's own money.

105
00:05:02,854 --> 00:05:04,123
Often, it's borrowed.

106
00:05:04,363 --> 00:05:06,203
Lines of credit from big banks.

107
00:05:06,203 --> 00:05:09,963
Or investors who buy bundles of these
loans, the same way investors buy

108
00:05:09,963 --> 00:05:11,403
bundles of mortgages.

109
00:05:11,403 --> 00:05:15,243
In the United States, many of these loans
aren't even made by the app you

110
00:05:15,243 --> 00:05:16,243
see.

111
00:05:15,811 --> 00:05:20,451
They're made by partner banks in the
background, and then passed on.

112
00:05:20,451 --> 00:05:23,971
That's why interest rates matter
so much to this business.

113
00:05:23,971 --> 00:05:28,035
When rates were near zero, borrowing the
cash for six weeks cost almost

114
00:05:28,035 --> 00:05:29,035
nothing.

115
00:05:28,675 --> 00:05:31,875
When rates went up, the cash in the
middle became expensive.

116
00:05:31,875 --> 00:05:34,755
And the free part of the
business got squeezed.

117
00:05:35,396 --> 00:05:36,946
Klarna went a step further.

118
00:05:36,946 --> 00:05:40,946
It has a banking license in Sweden, and
it takes deposits from savers.

119
00:05:40,946 --> 00:05:44,305
Part of the money you borrow at checkout
can come from someone else's savings

120
00:05:44,305 --> 00:05:45,320
account.

121
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:48,280
And then there's the other
half of the business.

122
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:50,360
The part that isn't free.

123
00:05:50,680 --> 00:05:52,671
Not every plan is pay in four.

124
00:05:52,671 --> 00:05:56,751
For bigger purchases, these
companies also offer longer loans.

125
00:05:56,751 --> 00:05:57,751
Twelve months.

126
00:05:57,631 --> 00:05:58,831
Twenty-four months.

127
00:05:58,831 --> 00:05:59,951
Sometimes longer.

128
00:05:59,951 --> 00:06:01,586
And those can come with interest.

129
00:06:01,746 --> 00:06:06,466
Affirm says its rates go up to
thirty-six percent a year.

130
00:06:06,706 --> 00:06:08,306
Here's what that can look like.

131
00:06:08,306 --> 00:06:12,386
Take a twelve-hundred-dollar laptop, paid
over twelve months at thirty percent

132
00:06:12,386 --> 00:06:13,386
a year.

133
00:06:12,977 --> 00:06:16,497
By our math, that's about two
hundred dollars in interest.

134
00:06:16,497 --> 00:06:19,377
The same laptop, with the same
button at checkout.

135
00:06:19,377 --> 00:06:21,297
Just a different plan.

136
00:06:21,617 --> 00:06:24,883
For Affirm, this is now the
biggest part of the money.

137
00:06:25,043 --> 00:06:30,003
In its 2025 fiscal year, Affirm reported
about one point six billion dollars

138
00:06:30,003 --> 00:06:31,283
in interest income.

139
00:06:31,283 --> 00:06:34,487
Its fees from merchants were about
eight hundred and eighty million.

140
00:06:34,646 --> 00:06:38,086
It also earned about three hundred and
eighty million dollars by selling

141
00:06:38,086 --> 00:06:39,846
loans to investors.

142
00:06:40,487 --> 00:06:43,766
So there are really two businesses
hiding behind the same button.

143
00:06:43,766 --> 00:06:45,948
The free one, paid for by stores.

144
00:06:46,187 --> 00:06:48,987
And the paid one, paid for by borrowers.

145
00:06:49,707 --> 00:06:50,707
Quick thing.

146
00:06:50,587 --> 00:06:53,307
If you like videos that follow
the money, subscribe.

147
00:06:53,307 --> 00:06:54,307
It's free.

148
00:06:54,107 --> 00:06:57,707
And you can pay for it in four
installments of zero.

149
00:06:58,715 --> 00:07:01,515
Part 4 The People Who Pay Late

150
00:07:01,515 --> 00:07:03,435
Now, the obvious suspect.

151
00:07:03,435 --> 00:07:04,635
Late fees.

152
00:07:04,635 --> 00:07:07,676
If you miss a payment, many
plans charge a fee.

153
00:07:07,676 --> 00:07:12,724
In its 2022 report, the CFPB found
typical late fees of around seven to

154
00:07:12,724 --> 00:07:13,724
eight dollars.

155
00:07:13,684 --> 00:07:17,124
Some companies charge no late fees at all.

156
00:07:17,124 --> 00:07:20,468
You might think late fees are the secret
engine of the whole industry.

157
00:07:20,628 --> 00:07:22,628
The data says they're not.

158
00:07:22,628 --> 00:07:27,588
In a 2025 report, the CFPB looked at
data from six big providers.

159
00:07:27,588 --> 00:07:31,648
In 2023, only about four percent of
loans were charged a late fee.

160
00:07:31,648 --> 00:07:35,727
And late fees brought in less than one
fifth of one percent of the money

161
00:07:35,727 --> 00:07:37,808
that went through these companies.

162
00:07:38,207 --> 00:07:39,872
Losses were going down, too.

163
00:07:39,872 --> 00:07:43,232
The same report found that the share of
loans that were never paid back

164
00:07:43,232 --> 00:07:44,832
fell in 2023.

165
00:07:45,792 --> 00:07:48,032
So late fees aren't where the money is.

166
00:07:48,032 --> 00:07:50,749
But late payments are
still a warning sign.

167
00:07:50,909 --> 00:07:56,029
In LendingTree's 2026 survey,
forty-seven percent of buy now, pay later

168
00:07:56,029 --> 00:07:59,381
users said they had paid late at
least once in the past year.

169
00:07:59,621 --> 00:08:02,981
Most said they were only a
few days behind.

170
00:08:03,541 --> 00:08:07,221
The bigger worry is something
critics call phantom debt.

171
00:08:07,221 --> 00:08:08,901
Each plan looks small.

172
00:08:08,901 --> 00:08:10,262
One hundred dollars here.

173
00:08:10,262 --> 00:08:11,262
Fifty there.

174
00:08:11,432 --> 00:08:14,706
But people can have several at once,
with different companies.

175
00:08:14,866 --> 00:08:18,786
And for years, most of those loans
didn't show up on credit reports.

176
00:08:18,786 --> 00:08:23,986
So no lender, and sometimes not even the
borrower, could see the full picture.

177
00:08:24,386 --> 00:08:26,007
That's starting to change.

178
00:08:26,007 --> 00:08:31,047
In 2025, FICO launched new credit scores
that include buy now, pay later

179
00:08:31,047 --> 00:08:32,047
data.

180
00:08:31,687 --> 00:08:33,767
Paying on time could help a score.

181
00:08:33,767 --> 00:08:35,242
Paying late could hurt it.

182
00:08:35,402 --> 00:08:39,162
Whether lenders actually use those
new scores is up to them.

183
00:08:39,162 --> 00:08:44,122
But the days of completely invisible
installment loans may be ending.

184
00:08:44,522 --> 00:08:46,706
To be fair, there's another side.

185
00:08:46,866 --> 00:08:52,066
For someone who pays on time, pay in
four really can cost nothing.

186
00:08:52,066 --> 00:08:55,906
Compared with a credit card that charges
more than twenty percent interest,

187
00:08:55,906 --> 00:08:57,948
it can even be the cheaper option.

188
00:08:58,107 --> 00:09:00,347
The risk isn't the price.

189
00:09:00,347 --> 00:09:03,227
It's how easy it is to say yes.

190
00:09:04,187 --> 00:09:06,521
Part 5 Rules That Came and Went

191
00:09:06,681 --> 00:09:10,761
For years, buy now, pay later
lived in a gap in the rules.

192
00:09:10,761 --> 00:09:12,361
It wasn't quite a credit card.

193
00:09:12,361 --> 00:09:14,713
It wasn't quite a traditional loan.

194
00:09:15,274 --> 00:09:20,473
In May 2024, the CFPB decided it was
close enough to a credit card.

195
00:09:20,473 --> 00:09:24,160
It said these companies had to give
users some of the same protections.

196
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:28,320
Like the right to dispute a charge, and
to get refunds handled properly.

197
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:32,320
A year later, under new leadership,
the CFPB withdrew that rule.

198
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:34,597
And said it wouldn't issue a new one.

199
00:09:34,916 --> 00:09:36,996
The UK went the other way.

200
00:09:36,996 --> 00:09:42,597
Since July 15, 2026, buy now, pay later
companies there must be regulated

201
00:09:42,597 --> 00:09:44,454
by the Financial Conduct Authority.

202
00:09:44,454 --> 00:09:47,894
They have to check whether customers can
afford what they're borrowing.

203
00:09:47,894 --> 00:09:51,734
They also have to give clearer
information up front, and help customers

204
00:09:51,734 --> 00:09:53,426
who fall into financial trouble.

205
00:09:53,426 --> 00:09:59,186
The FCA says the UK market was worth about
thirteen billion pounds in 2024.

206
00:10:00,226 --> 00:10:03,906
So, depending on where you live, the same
button can come with very different

207
00:10:03,906 --> 00:10:04,906
protections.

208
00:10:04,828 --> 00:10:07,867
In London, the lender has to ask
if you can afford it.

209
00:10:07,867 --> 00:10:11,227
In New York, that's mostly
up to the company.

210
00:10:11,467 --> 00:10:13,548
Meanwhile, the business kept changing.

211
00:10:13,548 --> 00:10:14,737
Even Apple tried it.

212
00:10:14,897 --> 00:10:18,657
Apple Pay Later launched in the
United States in 2023.

213
00:10:18,657 --> 00:10:22,817
About a year later, Apple shut it down, and
moved to offering other companies'

214
00:10:22,817 --> 00:10:24,905
installment plans instead.

215
00:10:25,385 --> 00:10:30,346
In 2021, at the height of the boom, Klarna
was valued at more than forty-five

216
00:10:30,346 --> 00:10:31,506
billion dollars.

217
00:10:31,506 --> 00:10:35,826
When it finally went public in New York
in September 2025, it was valued

218
00:10:35,826 --> 00:10:37,426
at about fifteen billion.

219
00:10:37,426 --> 00:10:39,026
A third of its old price.

220
00:10:39,026 --> 00:10:42,950
On its first day of trading, the shares
still jumped about fifteen percent.

221
00:10:42,950 --> 00:10:46,790
The business was smaller than the
hype, but it was real.

222
00:10:46,870 --> 00:10:48,710
Investors had learned something.

223
00:10:48,710 --> 00:10:51,910
Lending money for free is a great
business when interest rates are near

224
00:10:51,910 --> 00:10:52,910
zero.

225
00:10:52,492 --> 00:10:55,931
When rates go up, the money
you lend costs more.

226
00:10:55,931 --> 00:10:59,371
And the store's fee has to
cover a lot more.

227
00:11:00,251 --> 00:11:02,448
Part 6 So, Who Pays?

228
00:11:02,607 --> 00:11:05,567
Let's go back to our
four-hundred-dollar jacket.

229
00:11:06,047 --> 00:11:07,967
You pay four hundred dollars.

230
00:11:07,967 --> 00:11:10,207
Not a cent more, if you pay on time.

231
00:11:10,207 --> 00:11:13,728
The store pays a fee, maybe ten
dollars, maybe more.

232
00:11:13,888 --> 00:11:17,088
And that cost gets spread into
its prices for everyone.

233
00:11:17,088 --> 00:11:21,968
Banks, investors and savers put up the
cash, and get paid back with interest.

234
00:11:21,968 --> 00:11:23,874
And some shoppers pay late fees.

235
00:11:24,034 --> 00:11:26,274
But less than you'd think.

236
00:11:26,274 --> 00:11:28,727
The real money is on the other
side of the button.

237
00:11:28,886 --> 00:11:30,087
The longer loans.

238
00:11:30,087 --> 00:11:31,366
The ones with interest.

239
00:11:31,366 --> 00:11:35,046
And in the hope that once you've said yes
to four little payments, you'll

240
00:11:35,046 --> 00:11:36,966
say yes again.

241
00:11:37,286 --> 00:11:39,557
That's the quiet shift in this business.

242
00:11:39,557 --> 00:11:42,116
The apps don't just sit at
checkout anymore.

243
00:11:42,116 --> 00:11:44,116
They want to be where you start shopping.

244
00:11:44,116 --> 00:11:48,159
A place to browse deals, track orders,
and split the next purchase.

245
00:11:48,319 --> 00:11:51,839
